  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through next Monday)\nIssued at 1212 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nBy Thurs morning, the current trough over the western US will lift \ntowards the Great Lakes, but the pattern is quick to reload with \nanother closed upper low pushing onto the west coast. This will \nresult in renewed sfc pressure falls in the lee of the Rockies, \nkeeping southerly sfc winds in place locally. A modest warming \ntrend is anticipated during the Thurs-Sat period, with highs in \nthe upper 50s to mid 60s on the Caprock and low 60s to near 70F \noff the Caprock. Lows also remain seasonably mild, in the \nmid/upper 40s on the Caprock and low/mid 50s off the Caprock. \nTypically, lows are in the mid 30s by this time of year. \n\nThat next trough is set to eject onto the Plains by Sat night, \nsending a cold front through the forecast area, although \ndifferences remain in the strength and timing of the trough and \nassociated front. Precip can't be ruled out, but chances look\nquite low overall (less than 15%) as the airmass remains \nrelatively dry (PWATs less than 0.8\"). Despite the timing \ndifferences, ensemble guidance shows good agreement that cooler \ntemps will be in place for the start of next week, with highs \nlikely to cool at least into the 50s, if not upper 40s. Lows will\nalso be closer to seasonable values, and 20-40% of ensemble\nmembers have sub-freezing lows in areas from Lubbock to the north\nand west each morning Sunday through Tuesday. The ensemble mean \nstill keeps the lows a tad above freezing, in the mid 30s. \n\nThe upper pattern remains progressive, with another southern\nstream wave set to bring precip chances to W TX in the Mon-Tues \ntimeframe (40-60% chance). At this stage, guidance suggests the wet-\nbulb temperature profile remains above freezing, keeping any \nprecipitation as rain. The official forecast reflects this \nexpectation as well, but its at least worth checking back for \nfuture updates as this early week system comes into better focus.",
